      "id": 3,
      "title": "Section 3: Engineering Marvels of Ancient Roman Aqueducts",
      "text": "Paragraph A\nThe Architectural Marvels of Ancient Roman Aqueducts\nWater supply engineering reached unprecedented heights during the Roman Empire. Spanning thousands of kilometers across Europe, North Africa, and the Middle East, Roman aqueducts channeled fresh spring water from distant mountain sources into dense urban cities. Rather than relying on mechanical pumps, Roman hydraulic engineers utilized gravity alone, designing channels with precise, minimal slopes that maintained steady water velocity while preventing erosion of stone masonry.\n\nParagraph B\nTo cross deep valleys and gorges, engineers constructed grand multi-tiered stone arch bridges, such as the famous Pont du Gard in southern France. These monumental structures featured precisely cut limestone blocks assembled without mortar, held together by exact stone joinery and iron clamps. Where terrain permitted, channels were carved underground through tunnels to protect water purity and prevent evaporation under intense Mediterranean heat.\n\nParagraph C\nUpon reaching city outskirts, aqueduct water discharged into large distribution basins called castella aquae. From these central reservoirs, lead and terracotta pipes distributed pressurized water to public fountains, public bath complexes, and private residences of wealthy citizens. Historical estimates suggest that ancient Rome received over one million cubic meters of fresh water daily, a volume per capita that rivals modern metropolitan municipal supplies.",
